  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nBreezy northeasterly winds of 15-20 mph and mostly cloudy skies are \nexpected today over South Texas, following the cold front yesterday. \nA few sprinkles may be felt across the Coastal Plains this morning, \nbut majority of the area will stay completely dry in terms of new \nrainfall. Low clouds and offshore flow will limit diurnal heating, \nkeeping max temperatures today in the upper 50s to lower 60s. A few \nnorthern areas inland may reach the upper 40s for their low \ntemperature tonight!\n\nRain-free conditions will persist through midweek, before onshore \nflow returns during the latter half of the week. Moisture will creep \nup to near or above normal this upcoming weekend, mainly focused \nbelow 700mb. This moisture will combine with a series of mid-level \nshortwaves to warrant low (20-40%) chance of showers and \nthunderstorms Saturday through Monday.\n\nMinor coastal flooding and a high rip current risk will now be our \nprimary concerns this week as long swell periods of 7-9 seconds and \nstrong northeasterly flow enhances the Ekman transport mechanism. \nWater level guidance shows a significant spread in tide levels \nheading into the middle and latter part of this week, but still \nraises concerns of prolonged hazardous beach conditions.",
